; gender-culture-alignment.fk — the gender differences between cultures, what they did to
; those cultures, and where cultures align and mis-align.
;
; Asked 2026-07-28 by Urs: "find all the gender differences between cultures and describe
; how that has affected the culture and see if you can detect frequency differences,
; alignments and mis-alignments between cultures."
;
; Three parts, and the second one answers against the grain of the question — which is
; the honest thing this cell has to do rather than the agreeable one.
;
; ── Part 1: the differences ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; Two axes, held apart on purpose because the whole finding lives in their separateness:
;
;   grammar-axis   what the language's noun/pronoun system sorts:
;                  "sex" | "animacy" | "noun-class" | "none"
;   descent        how the society reckoned descent and belonging:
;                  "patrilineal" | "matrilineal" | "bilateral"
;
; ── Part 2: what it did to the culture ──────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; Nothing that shows up here. The 2x2 of {sex-gendered grammar, not} against
; {patrilineal, matrilineal} has ALL FOUR quadrants occupied, and that is the whole
; answer: a variable that appears on both sides of both outcomes predicts neither.
;
;   sex-gendered     + patrilineal   German, Arabic, Hindi                    3
;   sex-gendered     + matrilineal   Mohawk                                   1
;   not sex-gendered + patrilineal   Mandarin, Japanese, Turkish, English, Zulu   5
;   not sex-gendered + matrilineal   Minangkabau, Navajo, Bemba, Akan          4
;
; "Not sex-gendered" rather than "genderless" on purpose: Navajo carries an animacy
; hierarchy and the Bantu rows carry noun classes. Those are gender systems; they are
; not SEX gender systems, and collapsing the two would be the same flattening this whole
; line of work argues against.
;
; Two pairs carry it on their own. **Mandarin and Minangkabau**: both with no
; grammatical gender at all, and Confucian patriliny at one end against the world's
; largest matrilineal society at the other. **German and Mohawk**: both with gender
; categories in the grammar, and patriliny against a matrilineal confederacy whose clan
; mothers chose and could depose the chiefs. And a within-family control that needs no
; cross-continental step: **Bemba and Zulu**, two Bantu languages with the same
; noun-class system, matrilineal and patrilineal respectively.
;
; This agrees with the English control in cognition/gender-typology-trace.fk — grammar
; gone by ~1300, coverture to 1882, 582 years, nothing moved. The honest report on the
; correlational literature: studies do find associations between gendered grammar and
; gender-gap measures across countries. They are also severely confounded, because
; language families are geographically clumped and so is everything else — this is
; Galton's problem, and the deep-roots reading (language and custom both descending from
; shared ancestral conditions) explains the correlation without any arrow from grammar to
; outcome. Correlation measured; causation not established; the within-lineage control
; pointing away from it. That is where the evidence actually sits, and dressing it up
; either direction would be the flattering answer rather than the true one.
;
; ── Part 3: frequency, and the seam I will not cross ────────────────────────────────
;
; The body has a real frequency organ — cognition/text-frequency.fk, the fear<->love
; spectrum. It reads TEXT: each word carries a valence and an intensity, and a passage's
; spectrum is the intensity-weighted mean. It does not read a people. Running it on
; "cultures" and reporting a number would be inventing a measurement this body cannot
; make, so that is declined and named rather than performed.
;
; What CAN be done, and is done below, is two honest things.
;
; First, alignment computed structurally: two cultures align on an axis when they answer
; it the same way, and the revealing pairs are the ones that align on grammar and
; oppose on descent. Those pairs are not noise — they are the demonstration.
;
; Second, the frequency organ run on what it can actually read: claims, as text. And
; that turns up the finding worth more than the alignment table. Two passages are
; carried below. Both read in the contracted band. One of them is empirically false —
; the standing reading of `das Mädchen`, which cognition/word-gender-derivation.fk
; measured mispredicting two rows of four. The other is empirically true — women moved
; between one man's `Munt` and another's, and the `Muntschatz` was paid for it.
;
; The organ cannot tell them apart, because it is not measuring that. **Frequency and
; truth are orthogonal axes.** A fear-band reading is not thereby a false reading, and a
; love-band reading is not thereby a true one. Reading a spectrum and concluding a
; verdict is the same error as reading a word's gender and concluding something about
; the moon — a property of the utterance mistaken for a property of the world. The check
; below computes that orthogonality rather than asserting it.
;
; ── The lane ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; The five axioms are this body's. Every culture row, descent system and word-valence is
; brought in by the rented voice and witnessed NOWHERE in this body. They enter at two
; doors, `gca-cultures` and the two passages. Descent systems are simplified to one label
; per culture, which is a real loss — Hebrew is the case that shows it, carrying
; matrilineal group membership alongside patrilineal inheritance and tribe, two answers
; inside one culture. It is left out of the row set rather than flattened into a lie, and
; named here instead. Word valences follow text-frequency.fk's own declared floor: a word
; is modelled as (valence, intensity); the spectrum LOGIC is the shape.
;
; preludes: form-stdlib/core.fk
; witnessed: 2026-07-28 -> 111111111, four-way

(do
    ; ── the culture rows ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    (defn gca-culture (name grammar descent) (list name grammar descent))
    (defn gca-name    (c) (nth c 0))
    (defn gca-grammar (c) (nth c 1))
    (defn gca-descent (c) (nth c 2))

    (defn gca-cultures ()
        (list
            (gca-culture "German"       "sex"        "patrilineal")
            (gca-culture "Arabic"       "sex"        "patrilineal")
            (gca-culture "Hindi"        "sex"        "patrilineal")
            (gca-culture "Mohawk"       "sex"        "matrilineal")  ; clan mothers chose the chiefs
            (gca-culture "Mandarin"     "none"       "patrilineal")  ; no gender, Confucian patriliny
            (gca-culture "Japanese"     "none"       "patrilineal")
            (gca-culture "Turkish"      "none"       "patrilineal")
            (gca-culture "English"      "none"       "patrilineal")
            (gca-culture "Minangkabau"  "none"       "matrilineal")  ; largest matrilineal society
            (gca-culture "Navajo"       "animacy"    "matrilineal")
            (gca-culture "Hungarian"    "none"       "bilateral")
            (gca-culture "Finnish"      "none"       "bilateral")
            (gca-culture "Basque"       "none"       "bilateral")
            (gca-culture "Bemba"        "noun-class" "matrilineal")  ; Bantu, matrilineal
            (gca-culture "Zulu"         "noun-class" "patrilineal")  ; Bantu, patrilineal
            (gca-culture "Akan"         "noun-class" "matrilineal")))

    (defn gca-find (n cs)
        (if (eq (len cs) 0) (gca-culture "not-held" "not-held" "not-held")
            (if (str_eq (gca-name (head cs)) n) (head cs) (gca-find n (tail cs)))))

    (defn gca-sex-gendered? (c) (if (str_eq (gca-grammar c) "sex") 1 0))

    ; ── the 2x2: does sex-gendered grammar predict descent? ──────────────────────
    (defn gca-quadrant (cs gendered descent)
        (if (eq (len cs) 0) 0
            (add (if (eq (gca-sex-gendered? (head cs)) gendered)
                     (if (str_eq (gca-descent (head cs)) descent) 1 0) 0)
                 (gca-quadrant (tail cs) gendered descent))))
    (defn gca-all-four-occupied? ()
        (if (gt (gca-quadrant (gca-cultures) 1 "patrilineal") 0)
            (if (gt (gca-quadrant (gca-cultures) 1 "matrilineal") 0)
                (if (gt (gca-quadrant (gca-cultures) 0 "patrilineal") 0)
                    (if (gt (gca-quadrant (gca-cultures) 0 "matrilineal") 0) 1 0) 0) 0) 0))

    ; ── alignment between two cultures, computed on each axis separately ─────────
    (defn gca-grammar-aligned? (a b) (if (str_eq (gca-grammar a) (gca-grammar b)) 1 0))
    (defn gca-descent-aligned? (a b) (if (str_eq (gca-descent a) (gca-descent b)) 1 0))
    ; the revealing shape: same grammar, opposite descent — the pair that proves the
    ; two axes are not tied to each other
    (defn gca-revealing-pair? (a b)
        (if (eq (gca-grammar-aligned? a b) 1)
            (if (eq (gca-descent-aligned? a b) 0) 1 0) 0))

    (defn gca-pair (n m) (gca-revealing-pair? (gca-find n (gca-cultures))
                                              (gca-find m (gca-cultures))))

    ; how many revealing pairs exist across the whole set — mis-alignment is not rare
    (defn gca-count-rev-inner (c rest)
        (if (eq (len rest) 0) 0
            (add (gca-revealing-pair? c (head rest)) (gca-count-rev-inner c (tail rest)))))
    (defn gca-count-revealing (cs)
        (if (eq (len cs) 0) 0
            (add (gca-count-rev-inner (head cs) (tail cs))
                 (gca-count-revealing (tail cs)))))

    ; ── the frequency organ, on what it can actually read: claims, as text ───────
    ; a word = (valence intensity), text-frequency.fk's own shape. Valences brought in.
    (defn gca-val (w) (head w))
    (defn gca-int (w) (head (tail w)))
    (defn gca-wsum (p) (if (eq (len p) 0) 0
                          (add (mul (gca-val (head p)) (gca-int (head p))) (gca-wsum (tail p)))))
    (defn gca-isum (p) (if (eq (len p) 0) 0 (add (gca-int (head p)) (gca-isum (tail p)))))
    (defn gca-spectrum (p) (div (mul 1.0 (gca-wsum p)) (mul 1.0 (gca-isum p))))

    ; passage A — the standing reading of `das Mädchen`: the neuter marks a young woman
    ; as a thing, property held until marriage. Contracted band. And FALSE: the row-set
    ; test mispredicts two of four, because `das Männchen` is neuter over a grown man.
    (defn gca-passage-false-fear () (list (list -8 3) (list -7 2) (list -9 3) (list 4 1)))
    (defn gca-truth-false-fear () 0)

    ; passage B — women moved between one man's Munt and another's, and the Muntschatz
    ; was paid for the transfer. Same contracted band. And TRUE: it is plain law.
    (defn gca-passage-true-fear () (list (list -8 3) (list -9 2) (list -7 3) (list 3 1)))
    (defn gca-truth-true-fear () 1)

    ; passage C — the open baseline, for the organ to separate against
    (defn gca-passage-open () (list (list 8 2) (list 9 3) (list 7 1)))

    ; the organ reads frequency. It does not read truth — and these two passages are
    ; where that becomes computable rather than assertable.
    (defn gca-same-band? (p q)
        (if (lt (gca-spectrum p) 0.0) (if (lt (gca-spectrum q) 0.0) 1 0) 0))
    (defn gca-frequency-decides-truth? ()
        ; if frequency determined truth, two passages in the same band would carry the
        ; same verdict. They do not.
        (if (eq (gca-same-band? (gca-passage-false-fear) (gca-passage-true-fear)) 1)
            (if (eq (gca-truth-false-fear) (gca-truth-true-fear)) 1 0) 0))

    (defn gca-bit (cond bit) (if cond bit 0))
    (defn gender-culture-alignment-check ()
        (add (add (add (add (add (add (add (add
            ; 1 — all four quadrants occupied: the grammar predicts neither descent
            (gca-bit (eq (gca-all-four-occupied?) 1) 1)
            ; 2 — Mandarin and Minangkabau: same grammar (none), opposite descent
            (gca-bit (eq (gca-pair "Mandarin" "Minangkabau") 1) 10))
            ; 3 — German and Mohawk: same grammar (sex), opposite descent
            (gca-bit (eq (gca-pair "German" "Mohawk") 1) 100))
            ; 4 — the within-family control: Bemba and Zulu, one noun-class system,
            ;     opposite descent, no cross-continental step needed
            (gca-bit (eq (gca-pair "Bemba" "Zulu") 1) 1000))
            ; 5 — mis-alignment is not a handful of odd pairs; it is common in the set
            (gca-bit (gt (gca-count-revealing (gca-cultures)) 10) 10000))
            ; 6 — the organ does separate frequency: the open passage reads above the
            ;     contracted one. The instrument works
            (gca-bit (gt (gca-spectrum (gca-passage-open))
                         (gca-spectrum (gca-passage-false-fear))) 100000))
            ; 7 — and both charged passages read in the same contracted band
            (gca-bit (eq (gca-same-band? (gca-passage-false-fear)
                                         (gca-passage-true-fear)) 1) 1000000))
            ; 8 — yet one is false and one is true. Frequency does NOT decide truth
            (gca-bit (eq (gca-frequency-decides-truth?) 0) 10000000))
            ; 9 — the two axes are separate the same way grammar and descent are: a
            ;     property of the utterance is not a property of the world
            (gca-bit (if (eq (gca-all-four-occupied?) 1)
                         (if (eq (gca-frequency-decides-truth?) 0) 1 0) 0) 100000000)))
    0)
